{
  "forkIdentifierEthereumMainnet": {
    "_info": {
      "comment": "Fork identifiers for Ethereum mainnet. This is one of the few things a network needs that the upstream corpus structurally cannot supply: ethereum/tests keys every expectation on a fork NAME and is network-agnostic, so it says what Byzantium rules do and never says that Ethereum mainnet is at Byzantium from block 4,370,000. A fork identifier is exactly that second statement.",
      "whyThisRepositoryAuthorsTHISAndNotEthereumsStateTests": "Ethereum's own corpus is alive, maintained, and vastly larger than anything authorable here, and it is the authority for the EVM. Re-authoring it would duplicate it badly and go stale. What it does not contain -- measured, not assumed -- is any fork-identifier vector and any network activation height. Those are network facts rather than rule facts, and they are what this directory holds.",
      "bothForkKindsAppear": "Ethereum's schedule changes shape at the Merge. Boundaries through Gray Glacier are BLOCK numbers; Shanghai, Cancun and Prague are TIMESTAMPS, and EIP-6122 extends the checksum to accumulate them after the block-based ones. Every vector therefore carries both a head and a time. Ethereum Classic has no time-based fork, so this is a shape its files never exercise -- do not assume a reader written against them handles it.",
      "theTwoHeightsThatCollapse": "Constantinople and Petersburg both activate at 7,280,000 and contribute ONE entry to the checksum, not two. EIP-155 and EIP-158 likewise share 2,675,000. A client that folds a duplicate height in twice produces a wrong identifier from that point on and will not peer.",
      "divergenceFromEthereumClassic": {
        "whyThisIsStatedAsAnAssertionAndNotAsSharedFILES": "The two networks share a genesis and every rule through Homestead, so it is tempting to factor the agreement into a common directory. That is deliberately not done: a shared directory cannot say shared BY WHOM, and becomes a grab bag the moment a third network joins. The agreement is recorded here as a claim naming both networks and the exact head at which it ends, which degrades correctly -- at a future divergence you delete a sentence with a reason rather than quietly moving a file.",
        "identicalThrough": "head 1,149,999. Both networks compute 0xfc64ec04 with next=1,150,000, because they share Ethereum's genesis and neither has passed a fork.",
        "firstDivergence": "head 1,150,000. The fork HASH still agrees at 0x97c2c34c -- both passed Homestead and nothing else -- but the NEXT differs: Ethereum expects the DAO fork at 1,920,000 and Ethereum Classic expects Die Hard at 2,500,000.",
        "whatThatMeansInPractice": "The divergence is announced one fork before it happens. A node on either chain can tell the other apart at Homestead, before either has executed a block the other would reject. That is the fork identifier doing its job, and it is why the declined fork is visible in ../../ethereumclassic/mainnet/forkid/ as an absence rather than an entry."
      },
      "oracle": "Two independent sources agreeing. Computed from EIP-2124 and EIP-6122, and cross-checked against go-ethereum's OWN published mainnet table in core/forkid/forkid_test.go, which agrees on all 28 cases it covers including the block-to-timestamp transition.",
      "oracle-version": "go-ethereum, params/config.go MainnetChainConfig and core/forkid/forkid_test.go",
      "useYourOwnGenesisHash": "As with every file of this shape: seed the checksum from the genesis hash YOUR client computes and treat the one recorded here as a cross-check. Taken the other way, every vector passes regardless of what genesis your client built.",
      "generated": "2026-08-25"
    },
